  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Nurturing parents and children in Indonesia: an early-stimulation intervention</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To help Indonesian families nurture their young children (six months to three years old) for success and establish the evidence base for a cost-effective early childhood development program, the Abdul Latif Jameel Poverty Action Lab (J-PAL) Southeast Asia is adapting a program that was tested and successful in Jamaica called Reach Up and Learn. Reach Up and Learn has helped more than 100,000 children in 18 countries worldwide according to nearly 20 completed randomized controlled trials (RCTs) that show evidence of impact. In Reach Up and Learn, trained facilitators show parents in sessions how to use inexpensive and homemade toys, dolls, and picture books to stimulate and enrich their childrens development and build positive relationships. Under this adaptation, the trained facilitators will meet with small groups of parents to see how parents are using what they learn while at home, along with promoting fathers involvement. With support from Development Innovation Ventures, J-PAL Southeast Asia will partner with researchers from Rutgers University, Bangor University, and the National Team for the Acceleration of Poverty Reduction (TNP2K) to conduct a pilot RCT that compares effects between families in two treatment groups. Both will participate in group-based Reach Up and Learn; one will follow the traditional program and the other will receive extra support for fathers. A control group will receive the programs currently offered in the community (status quo). These findings will directly inform the design of a large-scale study and future rollout of Reach Up and Learn at scale. By testing the effectiveness of a lower cost, more easily scaled version of the proven Reach Up and Learn approach, J-PAL Southeast Asia has the potential to prove that this solution is an effective tool to improve early childhood development outcomes in Indonesia.</narrative>
  </description>
